# BananaOS Security Rule

Every application implements RBAC, audit logging, authentication, and input validation.

## RBAC Pattern (per app)

```typescript
// models/<App>Permission.ts
export type AppPermissionName = 'view_x' | 'edit_x' | 'delete_x' | ...;
export const USER_DEFAULT_PERMISSIONS: AppPermissionName[] = [...];
export const ADMIN_PERMISSIONS: AppPermissionName[] = [...];

// service
export async function hasPermission(userId, permission, userRole): Promise<boolean> {
  if (userRole === 'admin') return true;
  return !!(await Permission.findOne({ userId, permission, granted: true }));
}
export async function requirePermission(...) { ... }
```

## Audit Log Pattern

Log every mutation: user, action, entityType, entityId, deviceId, ipAddress, oldValue, newValue, reason.

```typescript
await logAudit(userId, 'contact_created', 'Contact', ctx, entityId, undefined, newValue);
```

## Controller Guards

```typescript
import { authenticate, requireAdmin } from '../middleware/auth';

router.get('/admin/audit', authenticate, requireAdmin, adminController.auditLogs);
router.post('/', authenticate, controller.create);  // + checkPerm inside
```

## Validation

Zod schemas in controllers mirror frontend test schemas in `__tests__/`. Never trust `req.body` without parsing.

## Middleware Stack

`helmet` · `cors` · `globalRateLimiter` · `authenticate` · per-route permission checks

## Session & Auth

JWT via `AuthRequest`. Pass `token` from `useAuthStore.getAccessToken()` on frontend. Include `X-App-Id` header for cross-app API calls.

## Don't

- Skip audit logs on admin actions
- Allow mutations without permission checks
- Expose internal errors to clients (use `AppError` with safe messages)
